### What is the remove Nth from end problem?

Given the head of a singly linked list, remove the Nth node from the end and return the new head. The challenge is that a singly linked list has no backward links, so you cannot count from the end directly. The two-pointer gap technique solves this in a single pass.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferNode {
    public String transferId;
    public BigDecimal amount;
    public TransferNode next;

    public TransferNode(String transferId, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.transferId = transferId;
        this.amount = amount;
    }
}
```

### How does the two-pointer gap technique work?

Create a dummy node before the head. Set both `fast` and `slow` to the dummy. Advance `fast` by N+1 steps. Then move both pointers one step at a time until `fast` becomes null. Now `slow` is the node just before the target. Unlink with `slow.next = slow.next.next`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TransferNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class RemoveNthFromEnd {
    public static TransferNode removeNthFromEnd(TransferNode head, int n) {
        TransferNode dummy = new TransferNode("DUMMY", BigDecimal.ZERO);
        dummy.next = head;

        TransferNode fast = dummy;
        TransferNode slow = dummy;

        // Advance fast by n+1 steps
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            fast = fast.next;
        }

        // Move both until fast is null
        while (fast != null) {
            fast = fast.next;
            slow = slow.next;
        }

        // Unlink the target node
        System.out.println("Removing: " + slow.next.transferId);
        slow.next = slow.next.next;

        return dummy.next;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TransferNode h = new TransferNode("TRF-001", new BigDecimal("10000"));
        h.next = new TransferNode("TRF-002", new BigDecimal("25000"));
        h.next.next = new TransferNode("TRF-003", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        h.next.next.next = new TransferNode("TRF-004", new BigDecimal("75000"));

        // Remove 2nd from end (TRF-003)
        TransferNode result = removeNthFromEnd(h, 2);
        TransferNode cur = result;
        while (cur != null) {
            System.out.println(cur.transferId + " : " + cur.amount);
            cur = cur.next;
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Removing: TRF-003
TRF-001 : 10000
TRF-002 : 25000
TRF-004 : 75000
```

### Why use a dummy head?

The dummy head handles the edge case where N equals the list length, meaning you must delete the head itself. Without the dummy, deleting the head requires a separate `if` branch. With the dummy, the same `slow.next = slow.next.next` code works for all positions.

```java
// Without dummy: special case needed when removing head
if (n == length) {
    return head.next; // skip the head
}

// With dummy: same code handles everything
TransferNode dummy = new TransferNode("DUMMY", BigDecimal.ZERO);
dummy.next = head;
// ... gap logic ...
slow.next = slow.next.next; // works even when removing head
return dummy.next;
```

### Why advance fast by N+1 instead of N?

Because both pointers start at the dummy (one node before the real head), advancing fast by N+1 creates the right gap so that when fast reaches null, slow is positioned one node before the target. If you advanced by only N, slow would land on the target itself, and in a singly linked list you cannot unlink a node you are standing on.

```text
  Dummy -> [1] -> [2] -> [3] -> [4] -> null
  n = 2

  After advancing fast by n+1 = 3 steps:
  slow = Dummy
  fast = [3]

  Move both until fast = null:
  slow = [2], fast = null

  slow.next = [3] which is the 2nd from end → remove it
```

### What about input validation?

If N is larger than the list length, advancing fast by N+1 steps runs past null and throws a `NullPointerException`. Production code should validate N.

```java
public static TransferNode removeNthSafe(TransferNode head, int n) {
    TransferNode dummy = new TransferNode("DUMMY", BigDecimal.ZERO);
    dummy.next = head;

    TransferNode fast = dummy;
    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
        if (fast == null) {
            System.out.println("N=" + n + " exceeds list length. No removal.");
            return head;
        }
        fast = fast.next;
    }

    TransferNode slow = dummy;
    while (fast != null) {
        fast = fast.next;
        slow = slow.next;
    }
    slow.next = slow.next.next;
    return dummy.next;
}
```

### How is this different from the two-pass approach?

The two-pass approach first counts the total length L, then walks to position L-N to find the node before the target. Both approaches are O(n) time and O(1) space, but the two-pointer gap does it in a single pass.

| Approach | Passes | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|---|
| Two-pointer gap | 1 | O(n) | O(1) |
| Count then walk | 2 | O(n) | O(1) |

```java
// Two-pass approach
public static TransferNode removeNthTwoPass(TransferNode head, int n) {
    int length = 0;
    TransferNode cur = head;
    while (cur != null) { length++; cur = cur.next; }

    TransferNode dummy = new TransferNode("DUMMY", BigDecimal.ZERO);
    dummy.next = head;
    cur = dummy;
    for (int i = 0; i < length - n; i++) {
        cur = cur.next;
    }
    cur.next = cur.next.next;
    return dummy.next;
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Single-node list (removing the only node), removing the head, and removing the tail are the key edge cases.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TransferNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class RemoveNthEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Single node: remove 1st from end
        TransferNode single = new TransferNode("TRF-001", new BigDecimal("1000"));
        TransferNode result = RemoveNthFromEnd.removeNthFromEnd(single, 1);
        System.out.println("Single removed: " + (result == null ? "empty list" : result.transferId));

        // Remove head (4th from end in a 4-node list)
        TransferNode h = new TransferNode("A", new BigDecimal("100"));
        h.next = new TransferNode("B", new BigDecimal("200"));
        h.next.next = new TransferNode("C", new BigDecimal("300"));
        h.next.next.next = new TransferNode("D", new BigDecimal("400"));
        result = RemoveNthFromEnd.removeNthFromEnd(h, 4);
        System.out.println("After removing head: " + result.transferId);
    }
}
```

```text
Removing: TRF-001
Single removed: empty list
Removing: A
After removing head: B
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The two-pointer gap is a pattern that extends beyond removal. The same gap idea finds the Kth node from the end, checks if a list has at least N nodes, and even helps with sliding window problems on linked lists.

In production at National Bank of India, Rajesh keeps a queue of scheduled future transfers. When he cancels the Nth-from-last transfer, the service validates N against the queue length during the fast-pointer advance phase, returning an error response rather than crashing if N is out of bounds.

```java
// Reuse the gap idea to find (not remove) the Nth from end
public static TransferNode findNthFromEnd(TransferNode head, int n) {
    TransferNode fast = head;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (fast == null) return null;
        fast = fast.next;
    }
    TransferNode slow = head;
    while (fast != null) {
        fast = fast.next;
        slow = slow.next;
    }
    return slow;
}
```
